windows.ts here is its source byte for byte except its three import lines:
the kernel's paths (../../kernel/calendar/{date,business,zoned}.ts) become
@hm/kernel/calendar (the same bytes; packages/kernel/VENDORED_FROM). It
imports no sibling, so nothing else came along. Nothing else may be edited:
src/__tests__/ei.test.ts compares the file with its import lines removed,
compares the import lines after that one rewrite, and fails on any
difference. Lint and Prettier skip this directory for the reason they skip
the vendored trees.

windows.ts is §11.1's and §11.2's Regulation X §1024.39 windows: one per
unpaid due date, with the live-contact leg due on day 36 and the written
notice leg on day 45; the payment that cancels them, the contact that
satisfies them, the sweep that breaches them; the bankruptcy, FDCPA-cease
and discharge overlays; the 180- and 190-day notice cycle and its cycle-end
review; and the once-per-case bankruptcy notice. It is pure: no clock, no
randomness, no I/O.

Not ported: ops.ts, ops-11-1.ts and plan.ts, which sit above it. The
servicing app has no fold that runs this engine over a loan's history (the
runtime's projectWindows does not call it: docs/servicing-engine-defects.md,
ei-2), so src/ei/grader.ts folds it as §11.1's and §11.2's state machines
say, and restates the rules outside it (the §1024.40 assignment, the
D2-2-02 outbound cadence and Reg F's call cap) with the lines they come
from; a test holds those lines to the servicing app's source.

To take a change from the servicing app, copy the file again and rewrite
the same three imports.
